# examples/side193 on a unit, reads only: is the stock software's copy
# of a loader, and the heap it goes over, on this unit what the
# emulator shows (docs/side193.md, "The heap under the loader's
# place"), and is the RAM a short program would be put in left alone?
# Run it against the emulator as well and compare the two results
# (bench193.py diff): the code should be the same where the builds are,
# the memory not.
#
# Rows of values are asked for a few at a time: version 1 of the
# resident loses every second frame of a long answer on a unit, and the
# tool asks again for each one that is missing.
info 0
info 1
note the OS module's header and the application's (their builds)
r32 80400200 24
r32 01c00000 24
note the application's routine that copies a loader and calls it (0x01c0a0e8-0x01c0a276 in V57510AA)
r32 01c0a0e0 68
note the loader's place: the module header and entries as they were copied
r32 a9b60000 8
r32 a9b60070 8
note where the copy ends in the emulator (0x09e3a378 for the stock length)
r32 a9e3a340 20
# (With the interface up before the disc went in, the emulator had at
# 0x09e836fc: ad010036 0012e620 a9fb1d1c a9d550dc 00000026, pictures'
# pixels of 7c1f after it.  The emulator's own run of this script, with
# the disc in from the start, comes before that heap is built and shows
# none of it.)
note the heap's block header the emulator has at 0x09e836f0, and what is around it
r32 a9e836c0 20
note pass 1: the RAM under the resident (0x0bfe0000-0x0bff0000), four words every 4 KiB
r32 abfe0000 4
r32 abfe1000 4
r32 abfe2000 4
r32 abfe3000 4
r32 abfe4000 4
r32 abfe5000 4
r32 abfe6000 4
r32 abfe7000 4
